\title{OPT-D: the price of a tick in a large session - a young and an old table for what a session finds again; the default cap gets 4 percent cheaper and stays where it is}
\taxon{Report}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{performance}
\tag{benchmark}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Optimisation package "OPT-D" of the wave after the first release, 2026-11-25 (the windows: 10:55 to 14:53 UTC). The lever is the third of [the profile of the final default configuration](rep-P11), section 6.3 and [its insight](ins-0IR8): a worker's session is reset at a cap of 1 MiB because a tick of the machine gets dearer as the session grows, and every reset throws away what a longer memory would save (a fifth of the ticks on navier-stokes-euler at 16 MiB). The task: find where the tick gets dearer, make a tick in a large session cost what it costs in a small one in the session tables of \code{rlvm-machine}, sweep the cap again and propose the cap that minimises the warm pass. This report is the measurement, the change, its gates and the sweep. The change is a patch against the main tree; nothing was published. No Oracle GraalVM was used.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Answer}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Where the tick gets dearer} (section 2). In the executable on navier-stokes-euler a tick costs 163 ns of worker CPU at the default cap, 194 ns at 4 MiB and 229 ns at 16 MiB. A little more than half of the increase is in the three tables that find something again (17 of 31 ns, 37 of 65 ns): their probes are as short and their load as low as at the default cap (0.3 to 0.36; 0.4 to 0.6 further slots per probe), but a session of 1 MiB with its tables is 1.4 MiB and a core's second-level cache is 2 MiB, so from the second megabyte on every probe is a load from outside it. Rehashing is 2.4 ns of a tick at the default cap and 7.5 ns at 16 MiB (0.8 and 1.5 records rehashed per record made); a reset is O(used), 14 microseconds for 20,000 records, 0.2 \% of the workers' time at every cap; fresh pages are 6 ns at 16 MiB. The other two fifths are not in any table: every line that reads a record, a run or a term gets slower with the size of what the session holds.}
\li{\strong{The change} (section 3): each of the three tables becomes a young table of fixed size, where every probe starts, and an old one that grows. A key that holds a record or a run made after the young table was last emptied can only name a young entry, so a probe goes on to the old table only for a key made of old things: with a young table of 16,384 records, 84 to 90 \% of the probes at the default cap and 60 to 70 \% at 16 MiB never leave it. The young tables are sized so that a session of 1 MiB never moves anything (2^16 record slots and 2^13 pairs instead of 2^15 and 2^12); a full young table is moved into the old one in batches whose loads overlap; an old table starts at the size the last session's ended with. Hash functions, records, ids and the footprint are what they were.}
\li{\strong{It answers as it did} (section 4): every counter of the machine is identical to the base's on the eight corpora with one worker, through the executable and through the JVM launcher (ticks, records, run words, pairs, resets, declines; 16 comparisons, no difference); an audit of the tables at every reset, which fails on a build with a deliberately wrong boundary, passes on the eight corpora at caps of 1, 4 and 16 MiB; the gates of the default configuration pass through the executable and through the jars with the counts of the release, the machine's own gate with its debug modes passes at the default cap and at 2, 4 and 8 MiB (at 16 MiB \code{memocheck} declines one declaration of Init with an internal error of the level store, in the pristine base exactly as in the candidate).}
\li{\strong{The guard at the present cap holds} (section 5). Executable: the geometric mean of the eight warm passes is 0.957 of the base's and every corpus is faster (Init 0.963, Std 0.968, cedar 0.988, reachability 0.845, con-leche 0.952, navier-stokes-euler 0.979, cslib 0.981, Mathlib 0.987, 14.81 to 14.62 s on a busy host; CPU ratios of all 24 rounds below 1). JVM launcher: 0.912 by the passes, 0.943 by the CPU, the largest ratio 0.999 (cslib). Peak resident memory is unchanged.}
\li{\strong{A tick in a large session is still dearer, and the cap stays at 1 MiB} (section 6). With the new tables the eight warm passes are in the geometric mean 1.5 \% longer at 2 MiB, 2.5 \% at 4 MiB, 5.8 \% at 8 MiB and 8.1 \% at 16 MiB than at 1 MiB, and at 2 MiB two corpora lose more than 3 \% (con-leche 5.0, Mathlib 3.8). navier-stokes-euler, the corpus whose ticks a longer memory saves, is level from 1 to 8 MiB (it lost 8.5 \% at 16 MiB before and loses 4.6 \% now); on cslib and Mathlib a larger cap costs what it cost before the change. The reasons, each measured: what a longer session finds, it finds old, and an old record is two to four loads from memory whichever table points to it; every record of a large session still has to be entered once into a table outside the cache (10 ns of a tick at 16 MiB, where the rehash it replaces was 7.5); and cslib and Mathlib rarely ask for old entries (8 to 9 \% of their closure probes at 16 MiB, 30 \% on navier-stokes-euler) while their sessions grow all the same. Raising the machine's cap alone, with the driver's left at 4 MiB, gives the same loss.}
\li{\strong{Verdict: GO for the tables} (one patch, \code{rlvm-machine} only), \strong{NO-GO for a change of the cap} (no second patch). The estimate of rep-P11 for this lever was a ceiling of 0.64 s on navier-stokes-euler, 0.26 s on cslib and 0.76 s on Mathlib if a tick at 16 MiB cost what it costs at 1 MiB, "expect half". What came of it is not that: the passes are 0.08, 0.10 and 0.19 s shorter in the guard window, all of it at the default cap and most of it from sessions that no longer rehash; the young and old tables are worth 16 \% on reachability, 3.6 \% on con-leche and 2.1 \% on cedar, where single units are large, and nothing on Std, navier-stokes-euler, cslib and Mathlib (section 5.3). The tick in a large session is still dearer. One thing is not settled: in the one window that compared the candidate with the base at the candidate's table sizes, Init's passes were 2 to 7 \% longer at the same CPU (three rounds), which the other windows and a look at its heaviest unit do not confirm. For an integrator who wants only what is certain on every corpus there is a smaller alternative, the table sizes alone (two lines; section 9).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. What was built and how it was measured}

\subtree{
\title{1.1 Trees and distributions}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Base.} A copy of the tracked sources of the main tree of 2026-11-25 10:50 UTC (the merged tree of [the first wave](rep-INT8): the inline list, the shared level base, the loader's stretches), built whole by \src{scripts/build.sh} with \code{\verb~|--dev~}: the executable (GraalVM CE 25.5.5.0.3 native image, \code{\verb~|-O3 --gc=serial~}, heap at most 13g, collection policy \code{\verb~|BySpaceAndTime~}, 269 forced and 81 never-inlined methods) and the JVM launcher of the same jars (machine heap \code{\verb~|-Xmx7g -Xmn1g~}). The sources of \code{rlvm-machine} in it are those of the main tree, file for file.}
\li{\strong{Candidate.} A second copy with the change of section 3, built the same way; only the machine jar differs from the base's. The inline lists have the same sizes (the one entry of the hand-kept list that named a method this change removes names its successor, section 9).}
\li{\strong{Instrumented build.} A third copy of the base whose probes count: per class of probe the probes, the further slots, the tag matches that are another key, the size and the load of the table, the age of what a hit finds, the entries moved by every growth, what every reset clears and how long it takes, and what a young window of 512 to 65,536 records would have answered (section 2.3). JVM launcher only; its times are not used, except the durations of the growths and the resets, which are the instrumented machine's own clock readings.}
\li{\strong{Symbolised images} of the base and of two stages of the candidate (the release flags plus \code{\verb~|-g~}), for the profiles.}
\li{No Oracle GraalVM was used. Nothing was installed into the shared Maven repository, no file of the main tree was edited, and nothing was published.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.2 Instruments}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Counters} of the instrumented build: one pass with 8 workers per session cap. A count does not depend on the load of the host.}
\li{\strong{Profiles.} The method and the tools of [rep-P11](rep-P11), section 1.3: \code{\verb~|perf record~} in the arena container, attached after the first of three passes, 8 workers; \code{\verb~|cpu-clock~} 1 ms, cycles, instructions, cache refills of the three levels, TLB walks, cycles stalled on memory; every sampled address resolved to its innermost inlined frame. New here: every event is divided by the ticks of the profiled passes, so a line reads "nanoseconds, misses per tick". An instruction count per method is not used: in memory-bound code the sample of an instruction counter lands behind the load the core was waiting for, like a cycle sample.}
\li{\strong{Timing.} Inside \src{bench/quiet.py} windows on the 8 measurement cores, as in [rep-OPT-A](rep-OPT-A): per corpus three interleaved rounds, in each round one process per variant, order rotated, \code{\verb~|rlvm[-jvm] --threads 8 --stats -X passes=N FILE~} and for a cap \code{\verb~|-X machine.session-kib=K -X session-mib=K/256~} (the driver's cap at four times the machine's, as rep-P11 ran it). Warm passes: passes 2 to N in the executable, the second half of the passes on the JVM launcher; per process the median of the warm passes (the mean under 0.5 s), per corpus the mean over the rounds.}
\li{\strong{Cost only.} Every corpus with one worker and \code{\verb~|-X stats-file~} through base and candidate, on both launchers: with one worker the machine's counters repeat exactly.}
\li{\strong{The table audit} (section 4.2), a check the change brings with it: with \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.tableaudit=true~} every reset checks the tables of the session that ends without using their probes.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1.3 The host was busy}

\p{Two to four other packages of the wave were computing during every window of this report, and this report's own gates ran on the efficiency cores; the load average was 12 to 60. A window keeps the 8 measurement cores for one process; the memory bandwidth and the two last-level caches of 8 MiB are shared with the other twelve cores. The base executable needs 14.8 s for a Mathlib pass in the guard window where [the integration of the first wave](rep-INT8) measured 13.11 s on a quiet host, and the same variant moves by up to 8 \% between two rounds of one window (section 6). Every ratio in this report is between processes that ran next to each other in one window; where a per-tick profile was taken in a disturbed window, the text says so. Nothing here is an absolute time for a table of benchmarks.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. Where the tick gets dearer}

\subtree{
\title{2.1 A session is larger than the cache of its core, and the cap of 1 MiB sits at that edge}

\p{The measurement cores are Cortex-X925 with a first-level data cache of 64 KiB and a second-level cache of 2 MiB each; there are two last-level caches of 8 MiB, each shared by ten cores, so a worker has one to three megabytes of it when the other cores are idle. A session that ends at the cap of 1 MiB holds 20,200 records (646 KiB), 93,500 run words (365 KiB), a record table of 2^16 slots after one doubling (256 KiB), a frame table of 64 KiB and a pair table of 128 KiB: 1.4 MiB that is read and written at random places. That fits the second-level cache, with nothing to spare. At a cap of 2 MiB the session with its tables is 2.6 MiB, at 4 MiB 4.8 MiB, at 16 MiB 10.5 MiB: from the second megabyte on a session does not fit, and what does not fit goes to a last-level cache that eight sessions share or to memory. The cap of [the earlier finding](ins-FJ5R) is the size of the second-level cache.}

\p{The machine's cap counts bytes in use, not capacity (32 bytes per record, 4 per run word, 20 per pair, 4 per table slot in use). On navier-stokes-euler a session cut at 16 MiB holds 142,000 records on average, not 300,000: sessions also end at every unit that is not accepted, at every step back in file order and at the driver's cap. On cslib and Mathlib that is what bounds them: at a cap of 16 MiB their sessions hold 62,000 and 70,000 records on average (19,000 at 1 MiB).}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.2 The base, tick by tick (the executable under perf)}

\p{navier-stokes-euler, the symbolised image of the base, 8 workers, the workers' samples of two profiled passes divided by their ticks (145.0, 125.9 and 115.7 M per pass). The passes of the three processes took 3.54 to 3.57 s, 3.65 to 3.67 s and 4.02 to 4.06 s, the times of [rep-P11](rep-P11) within 4 \%.}

