\ul{
\li{\strong{A tick is 31 ns dearer at 4 MiB and 65 ns at 16 MiB} (rep-P11: 29 and 58 ns), and the pass that saves 13 and 20 \% of the ticks is 3 and 14 \% longer.}
\li{\strong{The tables are a little more than half of the increase}: 17 of 31 ns and 37 of 65 ns. Their probes run at a mean load of 0.29 to 0.36 and look at 0.4 to 0.6 further slots (section 2.3): the probe sequences do not get longer, every load of them gets slower. A probe of a closure has 0.55 second-level refills at the default cap and 1.13 at 16 MiB, a probe of an application 0.50 and 1.18, with 0.16 and 0.20 last-level misses per probe where there were 0.04 and 0.05; a tick has 5.3 second-level refills at the default cap and 8.5 at 16 MiB.}
\li{\strong{Growth and rehash are 2.4 ns of a tick at the default cap and 7.5 ns at 16 MiB}: 1.5 \% and 3.3 \% of the workers' time (section 2.3 has the counts).}
\li{\strong{The kernel is 6 ns of the increase at 16 MiB}: slabs that outgrow the kept sizes (2^18 records, 2^20 run words) are new pages at every doubling.}
\li{\strong{The other two fifths are not in any table}: every line of the machine that reads a record, a run or an overlay term gets 5 to 25 \% slower as the session's slabs leave the cache (evaluation 20.1 to 22.4 ns, applications in inference 9.4 to 11.5, the type patterns 8.1 to 10.4). A layout of the tables cannot reach these.}
}
}

