\ul{
\li{\strong{Two probes per tick, and a third to a half of them end in a new entry.} 2.1 to 2.3 probes per tick; 31 \% of the closure probes, 43 \% of the application probes and 53 \% of the pair lookups find nothing at the default cap. A probe that finds nothing has touched one cache line of the table and no record.}
\li{\strong{The tables run half empty and the sequences are short}: mean load 0.29 at the default cap (a session of 20,000 records lives in a table of 2^15 slots until record 16,384 and in 2^16 slots after it) and 0.36 at 16 MiB; 0.44 to 0.60 further slots per probe; a tag of four bits sends one probe in thirty to forty to a record of another key. Nothing is wrong with the table as a hash table.}
\li{\strong{Rehash storms at the default cap.} The record table starts at 2^15 slots and a session that reaches the cap holds 20,000 records: 3,238 of the 3,461 sessions double it exactly once, each rehashing 16,384 records; the pair table (2^12 entries, 3,300 pairs per session) is doubled 3,607 times. Per record made, 0.83 records are rehashed at the default cap and 1.5 at 4 MiB and more; in the instrumented JVM the rehash loops take 0.44 s of a pass at the default cap and 0.95 s at 16 MiB (6.5 to 9.7 ns per entry moved).}
\li{\strong{A reset is O(used) and is not a cost}: 14 microseconds for a session of 20,000 records, 120 for one of 142,000; 0.65 to 0.84 ns per record at every cap; 0.04 to 0.05 s per pass over the eight workers, 0.2 \% of their time. The design holds: a grown table is zeroed whole (at most four slots per record), a table of base size by one fill or by one probe per record, the pair table through its log.}
\li{\strong{What a longer session is for}: at the default cap 91 \% of the closures that are found were made within the last 16,384 records; at 16 MiB 65 \%, and 23 \% are older than 65,536 records. On navier-stokes-euler the old entries are asked for, which is why the ticks fall. On cslib and Mathlib they are not: 98 \% of the found closures are younger than 16,384 records at the default cap and 91 to 92 \% at 16 MiB.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{2.4 What a young window would answer (the same counters)}

\p{Ids are given in creation order, and a key is made of ids that exist. So a key that holds a record or a run made after some moment T can only name an entry made after T (section 3.1). The instrumented build keeps such moments (the last multiple of S records, for S from 512 to 65,536), and counts for every probe whether its key holds something younger and where the entry was found:}

