\tr{\td{of the closure probes: an old key that is found in the old table, \%}\td{11.0}\td{18.0}\td{23.2}\td{27.0}\td{29.9}}
\tr{\td{of the closure probes: an old key that is nowhere, \%}\td{5.1}\td{5.2}\td{5.3}\td{5.4}\td{5.4}}
\tr{\td{young window of 65,536 records: closures, \%}\td{8.0}\td{7.5}\td{9.8}\td{19.4}\td{25.2}}
\tr{\td{neutral applications, \%}\td{2.0}\td{2.3}\td{5.5}\td{16.5}\td{22.7}}
\tr{\td{frames, \%}\td{3.0}\td{3.2}\td{7.0}\td{18.7}\td{25.6}}
\tr{\td{pair lookups, \%}\td{2.3}\td{2.5}\td{5.3}\td{14.8}\td{20.2}}
\tr{\td{probes of an old table per tick, all classes}\td{0.14}\td{0.13}\td{0.19}\td{0.42}\td{0.56}}
\tr{\td{keys with a young component that were found among older entries (must be 0)}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}\td{0}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The new keys are young keys.} With a window of 16,384 records at a cap of 16 MiB, 65 \% of the closure probes, 65 \% of the application probes and 70 \% of the pair lookups are settled by what was entered since the window began: either the entry is there or the key proves that it is nowhere. The probes of a table older than the window fall from 2.29 to 0.81 per tick; at 4 MiB from 2.22 to 0.63.}
\li{\strong{What is left for the old tables is what the session is kept for}: of the closure probes that go on, 30 points find an old record and 5 find nothing (16 MiB). A probe that finds an old record has to read that record, wherever a table keeps its slot.}
\li{\strong{The rule held in every probe}: no key with a young component was found among older entries, in 260 to 310 million probes per cap (it cannot be otherwise; the count is the test of the instrument).}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. The change}

\subtree{
\title{3.1 The rule: a key that holds something young names something young}

\p{Record ids and run ids are given in creation order, and a key is made of ids that exist when it is looked up: a closure or suspension is \code{\verb~|(term, frame, level substitution)~} with the id of a frame run; a neutral application is its head and its arguments; a free variable, a projection and a successor hold one value; a frame or a REST chunk is its slots and the id of its REST run; a pair is two values. So if a key holds a record or a run that was made after some moment T, an entry with that key, if there is one, was entered after T. This is the "young set" that the owner of the value records proposed after the reachability profile ([rep-machine-M1](rep-machine-M1), section 5.3) and that stayed the first open item of the machine's tables ([the cost of one machine operation](prob-28KQ)): "a lookup whose key holds a record id at or above the mark needs the young table only: absence is proven without the big table".}

\p{One step of it is not obvious and was a bug of the first draft, found by reading and then pinned by a test: an application that extends another one, \code{\verb~|mkApp~} of the value \code{\verb~|h a~} to \code{\verb~|b~}, has the key \code{\verb~|(h, a, b)~}. The value \code{\verb~|h a~} is not part of that key: the same application may have been made in one step before \code{\verb~|h a~} existed. So a young \code{\verb~|h a~} proves nothing; the head and every argument are tested (if \code{\verb~|h a~} is old, its head and its arguments are old, and only the new arguments need the test).}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.2 Two tables for each of the three}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Young.} \code{\verb~|vtab~}, \code{\verb~|rtab~} and \code{\verb~|pair~} keep their names, their word format and their hash functions and become tables of fixed size, the base size of \code{\verb~|Machine.Sizes~}. A young table holds what was entered since it was last emptied; the young pair table has a log of its entries (as the pair table had), so that it is cleared and moved in O(used).}
\li{\strong{Old.} \code{\verb~|vold~}, \code{\verb~|rold~}, \code{\verb~|pold~}: the tables that grow, in the same format. A session that never fills a young table has none: the field is then a shared one-word table that is never written, so a probe of "no old table" ends at its first load without a test.}
\li{\strong{Boundaries.} Each young table has its own, set when it is emptied: \code{\verb~|vY~} and \code{\verb~|rYv~} for the records (the next record id and the next run word at that moment), \code{\verb~|rY~} and \code{\verb~|vYr~} for the runs, \code{\verb~|vYp~} for the pairs. Three pairs of boundaries instead of one, because the three tables fill at different rates (0.48 records, 0.08 frames and 0.08 pairs per tick) and each should be emptied only when it is full. The test "this ref is a record at or above the boundary" is one subtraction and one shift, \code{\verb~|((ref - vY) >>> 28) == 0~}: an older record gives a negative difference, an immediate (bit 31) and a signal (0x7FFFFFFC and above) a difference with a bit above 27, and a record index is below 2^28.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.3 A probe}

\p{Every probe starts in the young table and is, up to the empty slot, the loop it was. At the empty slot it continues in a method of its own (\code{\verb~|Machine.oldRecord~}, \code{\verb~|Values.neuOld~}, \code{\verb~|Runs.pruneOld~}, \code{\verb~|Runs.internRunOld~}; the pair probes switch tables inside their loop): if the key holds something at or above the boundary, the entry is new; else the old table is probed with the same comparison, and only if that ends at an empty slot too is the entry new. A new entry is always written into the young table, at the empty slot the first half found. The second halves are separate methods because the step methods of the control convention must stay below 325 bytecodes (\code{\verb~|StepSizeTest~}), and because they are the cold half on the default cap.}

\p{\code{\verb~|Values.closureAt~} (a closure or suspension over the pruned working environment) loses its speculation. It used to load the slot that the record over a new frame would get before it probed the frame, so that two cache misses overlapped, and kept 256 recent frame hashes to predict when that pays ([rep-machine-M1](rep-machine-M1), D20). A new frame and the record over it now touch the young tables only, so the frame is probed first and, if it is new, the record is written at the first free slot of its sequence as before, without a comparison. The 256 hashes and the method \code{\verb~|closureOverNew~} are gone.}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.4 Moving a young table into the old one}

\ul{
\li{\strong{When.} When a young table is half full (the load of the tables stays at most 0.5, as it was): its entries are entered into the old table and it starts empty; its boundaries become the current ends of the slabs. The old table is made, or doubled, first if the session's entries would fill more than half of it; then every entry it held is entered again, as in the growth it replaces.}
\li{\strong{How.} In batches of 128 entries: a first loop computes the hashes and reads the slot each entry starts at, a second loop probes and writes. The loads of the first loop do not depend on each other, so the misses of a large table overlap; a loop that probes at once waits for a slot whenever the branch on its content was mispredicted. As lines of two profiles at a cap of 16 MiB: 6.9 ns per tick for the plain loop of a first build, 5.0 ns with the batches (two builds, the second in a disturbed window: an indication, not an A/B).}
\li{\strong{How large.} At least the size of the young table, at least what the session needs, and at least the size the old table of the last session had when it ended (clamped to the 4 MiB that the table pool keeps): sessions that end at a cap need the same table again, and a table that starts at its final size is never rehashed. With a cap of 16 MiB on navier-stokes-euler the process CPU of a pass is 1.3 to 2.8 \% lower with this than without (three rounds, each of them); at 4 MiB the rounds disagree; at the default cap it does not matter, because only the sessions of the largest units have old tables.}
\li{\strong{Refusals.} A growth that the heap refuses is what it was: \code{\verb~|E_MEM~} for the record table (and a \code{\verb~|HeapLimit~} if the young table would then pass three quarters, so that a probe always finds an empty slot), a \code{\verb~|HeapLimit~} for the other two; nothing is moved before the larger table exists.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.5 Reset, footprint, debug modes}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Reset} stays O(used): a young table is cleared by one fill or, if it holds less than a 32nd of its slots, by one probe per entry (the rule the base tables had); an old table goes back to its pool, where it is zeroed (at most four slots per record; eight if the size came from a larger last session); the pair tables are cleared through their logs. The boundaries return to the bases of the next session, also under \code{poison}, which moves the bases.}
\li{\strong{The footprint} is the same number: it counted the pair log's entries, which were the pairs; it now counts the pairs. So every reset happens where it happened.}
\li{\strong{The debug modes} see no difference; \code{memocheck} and \code{shortcheck}, which mask the status of a pair while they re-derive it, find the pair's entry in either table.}
\li{\strong{For tests and digests} the pairs of a session are enumerated in the order they were made, \code{\verb~|Machine.pairWord(i, word)~}: the old table's log, then the young one's. The digests of the M8 harness are the numbers they were.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.6 Base sizes}

\p{The young record table is 2^16 slots instead of 2^15 and the young pair table 2^13 entries instead of 2^12 (the frame table stays at 2^14 slots): 448 KiB for the three instead of 256 KiB. A session that ends at the cap of 1 MiB holds 20,200 records, 3,400 frames and 3,300 pairs, so with these sizes it never moves anything and never rehashes; the doubling at record 16,384 that nine sessions in ten paid (section 2.3) is gone. This alone, as a setting of the unchanged base (\code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.sizes=14,16,16,14,13,10~}), takes 1.0 to 3.7 \% of the process CPU of a pass on seven corpora (two interleaved rounds each: Init -3.6 \%, Std -3.7 \%, cedar -2.2 \%, reachability -2.2 \%, con-leche -2.2 \%, navier-stokes-euler -2.7 \%, cslib -1.0 \%; the two rounds of Mathlib disagree, -9.0 and +0.2 \%). The earlier measurement that set 2^15 ([session tables must start small](ins-8O66)) compared base sizes for sessions of one declaration; with warm sessions that run to the cap, the table a session ends with is the table it should start with.}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.7 What did not change}

\p{The hash functions, the table word (tag and index), the record and run layouts, the probe sequences inside a table, the order in which records, runs and pairs are made, and therefore every id: a record has the number it had. The slabs, their growth and their kept sizes. The session rules and both caps. No switch, no judgment.}
}

\subtree{
\title{3.8 What was looked at and left alone}

\ul{
\li{\strong{A recency cache in front of the old tables} for the hits on old entries. The finding of rep-machine-M1 stands: what a small cache of recent hits can answer is a slot whose line is still in the hardware cache; it was not built.}
\li{\strong{A denser old table} (a byte of tag per slot beside the slots, a load of 0.8): it would shorten the line a miss has to bring, not the miss, and a probe that finds its record reads the record anyway.}
\li{\strong{Larger kept slabs} (\code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.keep=20,22,20~}): no difference at the default cap; 4 \% of the CPU at a cap of 16 MiB (section 6.3). Not part of the patch, because the cap does not change.}
\li{\strong{Huge pages for the image's heap}: TLB walks are 0.2 per tick at the default cap and 0.5 at 16 MiB; another package of the wave works on them for the whole executable.}
\li{\strong{Counted probe loops} (the observation of [rep-OPT-A](rep-OPT-A) that a safepoint poll sits in every probe step): not tried; the hot instructions of a probe are its loads.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. That a probe answers as it did}

\p{A probe that skips the old table wrongly makes a second record, run or pair for a key that has one: two values that are the same value get two ids, and "equal ids" stops being "equal values". No timing shows that. Four nets, each able to catch it on its own:}

\subtree{
\title{4.1 Every counter identical, on eight corpora and both launchers}

\p{One worker, \code{\verb~|-X stats-file~}, base against candidate at the default cap, through the executable and through the JVM launcher: all 137 to 142 values of the machine's statistics are equal on each of the eight corpora on each launcher (16 comparisons, 0 differences), with the two columns the comparison of [rep-OPT-A](rep-OPT-A) left out, the bytes of the sessions at their resets. Mathlib: 574,100,326 ticks, 288,313,296 records, 1,255,939,258 run words, 32,500,348 pairs, 14,838 resets, 714,652 units accepted by the machine and 120 declined, on both sides. Init 34,490,709 ticks and 15,271,167 records, Std 56,957,035 and 26,050,886, cedar 76,668,269 and 36,675,335, reachability 9,014,777 and 4,807,871, con-leche 70,755,128 and 30,386,037, navier-stokes-euler 144,220,381 and 69,470,249, cslib 215,956,521 and 112,841,885. A second record for one key would change the record count; a pair entry that is not found would change the hits of conversion and the ticks.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.2 The table audit}

\p{\code{\verb~|Debug.tablesAudit~} checks the tables of a live session without their probes: it builds a map of every record key, every interned run and every pair of the session and fails on a second one; it looks every record, run and pair up in the table its id says and in the other one; it checks that nothing in an old table holds a record or a run from that table's boundary on (the rule of section 3.1 as a property of the data); and it compares the counters with the occupied slots. With \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.tableaudit=true~} every reset runs it and a failure ends the run with an \code{\verb~|AssertionError~} (exit status 3).}

\ul{
\li{\strong{It fails when it should.} A build in which the closure probe tests the frame against the wrong boundary (one of four deliberate faults) accepts Init without the audit and ends with "records 16366 and 16386 have one key (kind 7): I-ID is broken" with it.}
\li{\strong{It passes} on the eight corpora through the candidate's JVM launcher with 8 workers at the default cap, at 4 MiB and at 16 MiB (24 runs), and on navier-stokes-euler and cslib at 2 and 8 MiB: every session of every run audited at its reset, no failure, every verdict accept.}
\li{The M8 gate of the candidate ran with the audit on at every cap (section 4.4).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.3 The tests of the module}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The fast group}: 424 tests, 0 failures, 3 skipped (the base: 420, the same 3 skipped). Seven of them needed a change because they looked into the tables: the reference probes of the test tree (\code{\verb~|TestTables~}) now search both tables whatever the key holds, the pair digests of the M8 harness and of the tier comparison enumerate the pairs through \code{\verb~|Machine.pairWord~}, and two tests that handed the real pair probe invented record refs now make those records first: the probes are right only for keys made of records that exist, which is what the machine gives them.}
\li{\strong{A new class, \code{\verb~|YoungOldTablesTest~}} (4 tests): random sorts, closures over frames of existing values, variables, applications in one step and by extension, projections, REST chains with far frames, and pairs, checked against a model that knows the id of every key, on machines whose young tables hold 8, 4 and 4 entries (and 1 each), with the audit every 41 to 53 steps and resets in between; the standard sizes with three sessions of 150,000 operations each, which move into old tables of the size the session before ended with. It was run against four deliberate faults (the extension rule of section 3.1, a pair boundary off by three, frames tested against the records' boundary, closures against the runs') and fails on each of them.}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|SHostileSlowTest~}}: 2 tests, 0 failures (56 switch sets by 1,196 hostile exports).}
\li{\strong{\code{\verb~|StepSizeTest~}}: every step method within the limits of the control convention (a first draft with the old-table loop inside the probes was over them in four methods).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4.4 The gates}

\p{All on the candidate's private distribution (executable and jars of one build; the class files of the patch are byte for byte those of that build), every launcher without an option unless the row says so.}

\table{
\tr{\th{gate}\th{on what}\th{result}}
\tr{\td{the machine's fast group}\td{the candidate tree}\td{424 tests, 0 failures, 3 skipped}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|SHostileSlowTest~}}\td{the candidate tree}\td{2 tests, 0 failures: 56 switch sets by 1,196 hostile exports}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|scripts/gate.sh --defaults --corpus all --hostile --native~}}\td{the executable}\td{PASS. 230 of 230 tests correct, 0 declined; 17,775 mutants: SOUNDNESS 0, COMPLETENESS 0, agree 15,741, declined 2,034, 0 errors; the differential self-test caught its engine; hostile suite, 1,196 rows: agree 1,124, accepts of kind T tolerated 68, 0 wrong accept, 0 wrong reject, 4 declined}}
