}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The geometric mean is 0.912 by the passes and 0.943 by the process CPU; no corpus is slower}: the largest ratio is cslib's, 0.999 by the passes and 0.995 by the CPU (rounds 0.987, 0.994, 1.004). The window was the most disturbed of this report (single passes of the base of 5.3 s on navier-stokes-euler and 7.4 s on cslib among passes of 3.5 and 4.6 s), so the ratio of the passes overstates the gain on navier-stokes-euler and reachability; the CPU ratios are the steadier figure: Init 0.969, Std 0.953, cedar 0.980, reachability 0.808, con-leche 0.939, navier-stokes-euler 0.946, cslib 0.995, Mathlib 0.965 (94.9 to 91.6 s of CPU per pass, 12.56 to 12.05 s).}
\li{\strong{Peak resident memory} is within 6 \% (con-leche 1,913 and 1,808 MiB; Mathlib 5,116 and 5,119 MiB).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.3 The two parts apart: what the young and old tables add to the sizes alone}

\p{The change has two parts. The sizes of the young tables are a setting of the unchanged base (\code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.sizes=14,16,16,14,13,10~}: its one table per kind then starts where a session of 1 MiB ends, and the counters are identical, checked on Init and navier-stokes-euler). The young and old tables are the rest. The base executable with that setting against the candidate, one window, three interleaved rounds:}

\table{
\tr{\th{corpus}\th{rounds x passes}\th{base with the sizes: pass s}\th{candidate: pass s}\th{ratio}\th{base with the sizes: CPU s}\th{candidate: CPU s}\th{ratio}\th{CPU ratio of each round}\th{peak RSS MiB: base with the sizes}\th{candidate}}
\tr{\td{init}\td{3 x 8}\td{0.634}\td{0.659}\td{1.039}\td{4.55}\td{4.56}\td{1.001}\td{1.002 1.004 0.998}\td{380}\td{374}}
\tr{\td{std}\td{3 x 6}\td{1.136}\td{1.130}\td{0.995}\td{8.35}\td{8.29}\td{0.993}\td{0.995 0.992 0.993}\td{602}\td{595}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{3 x 6}\td{1.593}\td{1.560}\td{0.979}\td{11.74}\td{11.65}\td{0.992}\td{0.997 0.983 0.997}\td{759}\td{752}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{3 x 8}\td{0.538}\td{0.452}\td{0.840}\td{1.44}\td{1.22}\td{0.850}\td{0.847 0.850 0.853}\td{719}\td{734}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{3 x 6}\td{1.527}\td{1.473}\td{0.964}\td{9.81}\td{9.56}\td{0.975}\td{0.971 0.980 0.974}\td{1360}\td{1358}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{3 x 4}\td{3.840}\td{3.876}\td{1.009}\td{28.58}\td{28.55}\td{0.999}\td{0.989 1.010 0.998}\td{1486}\td{1476}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{3 x 4}\td{5.485}\td{5.443}\td{0.992}\td{39.77}\td{39.69}\td{0.998}\td{0.996 1.000 0.998}\td{1998}\td{2002}}
\tr{\td{mathlib}\td{3 x 3}\td{14.175}\td{14.200}\td{1.002}\td{108.75}\td{108.84}\td{1.001}\td{1.001 1.001 1.001}\td{4410}\td{4409}}
\tr{\td{\strong{geometric mean}}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{\strong{0.976}}\td{}\td{}\td{\strong{0.975}}\td{}\td{}\td{}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{On the corpora of small units the sizes are the whole gain.} Std, navier-stokes-euler, cslib and Mathlib are within 1 \% of the base with the sizes by the passes and within 0.7 \% by the CPU (Mathlib 1.001 in each of three rounds); what section 5.1 shows for them (2 to 3 \%) is the rehash that no longer happens.}
\li{\strong{The young and old tables are the gain where single units are large}: reachability 16 \% (CPU 15 \%, the three rounds within a point), con-leche 3.6 \% (CPU 2.5 \%), cedar 2.1 \% (CPU 0.8 \%). The geometric mean of the eight is 0.976 by the passes and 0.975 by the CPU.}
\li{\strong{Init}: the same CPU (1.001; rounds 1.002, 1.004, 0.998) and passes that are 2.2 to 7.0 \% longer in the three rounds (0.634 against 0.659 s). The window does not say why. The heaviest unit of Init (716,000 ticks, by itself a session several times a young table) is not slower with the new tables but faster, 60 against 84 ms in a one-worker run of its block of 1,024 units (a diagnosis on two spare cores, not a window), and the other 1,022 units of the block take 0.97 of their time; and the two other windows chain to 0.999 (candidate against base 0.963 by the passes, section 5.1; sizes against base 0.964, section 3.6). A window of six rounds for Init alone was queued behind the windows of other packages for more than an hour and given up, so whether the passes of Init are longer with the new tables than with the sizes alone is not settled; against the base they are 3.7 \% shorter (section 5.1).}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. Large sessions with the change: the sweep of the cap}

\subtree{
\title{6.1 The sweep}

\p{The candidate's executable, 8 workers, the cap of the machine at 1 MiB (no option), 2, 4, 8 and 16 MiB with the driver's cap at four times it, three interleaved rounds per corpus in one window; ratios against the default cap. Every one of the 120 processes accepted.}

\table{
\tr{\th{corpus}\th{rounds x passes}\th{1 MiB: pass s}\th{2 MiB}\th{4 MiB}\th{8 MiB}\th{16 MiB}\th{ratio to 1 MiB: 2}\th{4}\th{8}\th{16}\th{CPU ratio: 2}\th{4}\th{8}\th{16}\th{peak RSS MiB at 1 / 4 / 16 MiB}}
\tr{\td{init}\td{3 x 6}\td{0.651}\td{0.665}\td{0.665}\td{0.722}\td{0.709}\td{1.022}\td{1.022}\td{1.110}\td{1.090}\td{1.028}\td{1.048}\td{1.094}\td{1.100}\td{368 / 447 / 617}}
\tr{\td{std}\td{3 x 5}\td{1.128}\td{1.162}\td{1.209}\td{1.227}\td{1.273}\td{1.030}\td{1.072}\td{1.088}\td{1.129}\td{1.033}\td{1.062}\td{1.095}\td{1.123}\td{591 / 644 / 886}}
\tr{\td{cedar}\td{3 x 5}\td{1.606}\td{1.637}\td{1.687}\td{1.733}\td{1.780}\td{1.019}\td{1.051}\td{1.079}\td{1.109}\td{1.031}\td{1.058}\td{1.090}\td{1.113}\td{751 / 831 / 1050}}
\tr{\td{reachability}\td{3 x 6}\td{0.489}\td{0.464}\td{0.466}\td{0.471}\td{0.457}\td{0.948}\td{0.954}\td{0.964}\td{0.934}\td{1.024}\td{1.020}\td{1.029}\td{1.022}\td{740 / 783 / 801}}
\tr{\td{con-leche}\td{3 x 5}\td{1.492}\td{1.566}\td{1.548}\td{1.593}\td{1.652}\td{1.050}\td{1.038}\td{1.067}\td{1.107}\td{1.024}\td{1.043}\td{1.084}\td{1.127}\td{1336 / 1548 / 2094}}
\tr{\td{navier-stokes-euler}\td{3 x 4}\td{4.142}\td{4.091}\td{4.062}\td{4.116}\td{4.333}\td{0.988}\td{0.981}\td{0.994}\td{1.046}\td{1.009}\td{1.014}\td{1.029}\td{1.060}\td{1487 / 1702 / 2681}}
\tr{\td{cslib}\td{3 x 4}\td{5.406}\td{5.538}\td{5.676}\td{5.806}\td{5.930}\td{1.024}\td{1.050}\td{1.074}\td{1.097}\td{1.049}\td{1.078}\td{1.106}\td{1.131}\td{2031 / 2320 / 2803}}
\tr{\td{mathlib}\td{3 x 3}\td{14.736}\td{15.295}\td{15.329}\td{16.149}\td{16.918}\td{1.038}\td{1.040}\td{1.096}\td{1.148}\td{1.040}\td{1.072}\td{1.111}\td{1.150}\td{4399 / 4622 / 6653}}
\tr{\td{\strong{geometric mean}}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{\strong{1.015}}\td{\strong{1.025}}\td{\strong{1.058}}\td{\strong{1.081}}\td{\strong{1.030}}\td{\strong{1.049}}\td{\strong{1.079}}\td{\strong{1.102}}\td{}}
\tr{\td{largest ratio of a corpus}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{1.050}\td{1.072}\td{1.110}\td{1.148}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{}\td{}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The cap of 1 MiB minimises the warm pass.} The geometric mean of the eight passes is 1.5 \% higher at 2 MiB, 2.5 \% at 4 MiB, 5.8 \% at 8 MiB and 8.1 \% at 16 MiB; at 2 MiB con-leche loses 5.0 \% and Mathlib 3.8 \%, so a cap of 2 MiB fails the guard on two corpora and gains on none by more than the noise. No change of the cap is proposed, and there is no second patch.}
\li{\strong{navier-stokes-euler is level from 1 to 8 MiB}: the pass is 4.14, 4.09, 4.06 and 4.12 s, the process CPU 0.9, 1.4 and 2.9 \% above the default. This is the corpus where a longer memory saves the most (7, 13, 18 and 21 \% of the ticks, section 2.3), and with the new tables the saved ticks pay for the dearer ones, no more. reachability does not depend on the cap (its two units are sessions of their own; the CPU is within 3 \%, the passes of 0.46 to 0.49 s are within their noise).}
\li{\strong{Everywhere else a larger cap costs what it cost before the change.} rep-P11 measured the base at 4 and 16 MiB: cslib +5.3 and +9.7 \%, Mathlib +6.4 and +14.6 \%, navier-stokes-euler 0 and +8.5 \%. The candidate: cslib +5.0 and +9.7 \%, Mathlib +4.0 and +14.8 \%, navier-stokes-euler -1.9 and +4.6 \%. The penalty of the largest cap is halved where the old entries are asked for, and it does not move on cslib and Mathlib.}
\li{\strong{Peak resident memory} grows with the cap: Mathlib 4,399 MiB at a cap of 1 MiB, 4,622 at 4 MiB, 6,653 at 16 MiB; navier-stokes-euler 1,487, 1,702 and 2,681 MiB.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.2 Why the tick stays dearer: what is found old has to be read where it lies}

\p{navier-stokes-euler, the symbolised image of the candidate under perf at 1, 4 and 16 MiB, and of the base at 1 MiB, in one window; the window was busy (the base's tick is 180 ns in it and 163 ns in the window of section 2.2), so the columns compare with each other and not with that section.}

\table{
\tr{\th{per tick of the machine, workers}\th{base, 1 MiB}\th{candidate, 1 MiB}\th{candidate, 4 MiB}\th{candidate, 16 MiB}}
\tr{\td{\strong{CPU time, ns}}\td{\strong{180.3}}\td{\strong{176.0}}\td{\strong{208.8}}\td{\strong{235.8}}}
\tr{\td{second-level refills}\td{5.11}\td{4.81}\td{6.21}\td{7.83}}
\tr{\td{last-level read misses}\td{0.73}\td{0.72}\td{1.04}\td{1.44}}
\tr{\td{data TLB walks}\td{0.23}\td{0.23}\td{0.31}\td{0.46}}
\tr{\td{ns: the probes up to the end of the young table (closures, applications, frames, other records)}\td{42.1}\td{40.8}\td{50.2}\td{53.9}}
\tr{\td{ns: the probes' second halves in the old tables}\td{-}\td{1.6}\td{6.9}\td{13.8}}
\tr{\td{ns: moving young tables into old ones}\td{-}\td{1.5}\td{6.8}\td{10.2}}
\tr{\td{ns: growth and rehash}\td{3.3}\td{0.0}\td{0.1}\td{0.1}}
\tr{\td{\strong{ns: the intern tables together}}\td{\strong{45.4}}\td{\strong{43.9}}\td{\strong{63.9}}\td{\strong{78.0}}}
\tr{\td{ns: writing new records and runs}\td{5.5}\td{4.8}\td{5.2}\td{4.8}}
\tr{\td{ns: kernel}\td{2.3}\td{2.2}\td{3.2}\td{8.9}}
\tr{\td{ns: everything else}\td{127.2}\td{125.1}\td{136.5}\td{144.2}}
}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The tick of the candidate is 33 ns dearer at 4 MiB and 60 ns at 16 MiB than at 1 MiB}: the base's increase (31 and 65 ns in its own window), give or take what two windows resolve. The tables are 20 and 34 ns of it, as they were 17 and 37.}
\li{\strong{The cost moved, it did not go.} The probes of a table that is out of the cache went from 2.3 per tick to 0.8 (section 2.4). But each record of a large session is now entered twice, once into the young table and once, with a cache miss the first time could not have, into the old one: 10 ns per tick at 16 MiB, where the base's rehash was 7.5. Each probe that finds an old record pays for its slot and for the record as before: 14 ns per tick. And the first halves of the probes are 13 ns dearer although the young table is the same at every cap, because the young records and runs they compare against share the second-level cache with everything the session reads elsewhere: second-level refills go from 4.8 to 7.8 per tick.}
\li{\strong{What is found old has to be read where it lies.} A session of 16 MiB is asked for old records 0.2 times per tick by closures alone (section 2.4). Such a hit is a slot of the old table, the record, for an application its spine, and then whatever the caller reads through the record: its value, the frame of that value. None of these is in the cache of a core whose session is five times the cache, and no table layout brings them there. The hit saves a tick that would have cost 160 ns and costs two to four loads from memory.}
\li{\strong{On cslib and Mathlib there is little to find}: 8 to 9 \% of their closure probes find a record older than 16,384 records even at 16 MiB (30 \% on navier-stokes-euler; 4 to 5 \% and 11 \% at the default cap), their ticks fall by 4 to 6 \%, and the sessions get larger all the same (62,000 to 70,000 records at 16 MiB against 19,000). They pay for a large session and get nothing for it, whichever tables it has.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6.3 Two other suspects: the driver's cap, and the kept slabs}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The driver's cap is not what costs.} The sweep raised the driver's cap with the machine's (as rep-P11 did), so a worker's term and level overlays grew as well. The candidate with only the machine's cap raised, three interleaved rounds, ratios to the default: navier-stokes-euler 0.995 by the pass and 1.012 by the CPU at 2 MiB under the driver's 4 MiB (1.006 and 1.014 under 8 MiB) and 1.000 and 1.013 at 4 MiB under 4 MiB; cslib 1.038 and 1.039 (1.034 and 1.041), 1.051 and 1.061; Mathlib 1.027 and 1.028 (1.000 and 1.022), 1.013 and 1.037, the CPU above the default in every round. What a larger cap costs is in the machine's own session.}
\li{\strong{Larger kept slabs help the largest cap only.} A session's record and run slabs keep their capacity across resets up to 2^18 records and 2^20 run words; a slab beyond that is allocated anew, and its pages are new pages. With \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.keep=20,22,20~} at a cap of 16 MiB on navier-stokes-euler the process CPU of a pass is 4.0 \% lower (two rounds, 0.949 and 0.970) and the peak resident set is 2,057 instead of 2,432 MiB, because the outgrown slabs are no longer garbage: the kernel's 6 to 7 ns of a tick at that cap (sections 2.2 and 6.2) and the copies of the growing slabs are what it removes. At the default cap the setting changes nothing (1.001), and at 4 MiB, where few sessions outgrow the kept sizes, an earlier window showed none either. In that window the candidate at 16 MiB needs 8.5 \% more CPU than at 1 MiB without the setting and 4.1 \% with it (6.0 \% without it in the sweep); it does not move the verdict, which is decided at 2 and 4 MiB by cslib and Mathlib. A cap above 4 MiB, if one is ever wanted, should come with kept sizes that grow with it.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. The lever against its estimate, and what is left of it}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The estimate.} [rep-P11](rep-P11), section 6.3: a machine whose tick at 16 MiB costs what it costs at 1 MiB would save 0.64 s of the pass on navier-stokes-euler, 0.26 s on cslib and 0.76 s on Mathlib; half of that was expected of a first design, by a layout of the tables and by pooled slabs.}
\li{\strong{What the tables gave.} Nothing of that kind: the tick of a large session is as dear as it was (section 6.2), and the best cap is the old one. What they gave is at the default cap and was not in the estimate: 4.3 \% of the geometric mean of the eight passes in the executable (0.08 s on navier-stokes-euler, 0.10 s on cslib, 0.19 s on Mathlib in the guard window). Taken apart (section 5.3), the sizes of the young tables, with which a session of 1 MiB no longer rehashes, are the gain on the corpora of small units, and the young and old tables are the gain where single units are large: reachability 16 \%, con-leche 3.6 \%, cedar 2.1 \%.}
\li{\strong{Why the estimate was too high.} It took the whole increase of a tick for something a table layout addresses. Two fifths of the increase are not in the tables (section 2.2); of the tables' part, what a probe pays for an entry that is found old cannot be avoided by any table, and the rest - the probes that find nothing or something young - turned out to be replaceable only by other work of the same price, because a record of a large session must still be findable later and so must be written once to a place outside the cache. And on cslib and Mathlib a larger cap buys 4 to 6 \% of the ticks for sessions three times as large, whatever a tick costs.}
\li{\strong{What would have to be true for a larger cap to pay.} Either the old part of a session is small enough to stay in the cache, which a cap is; or it is asked for rarely and in bulk; or what it holds is denser. The machine keeps a value in 32 bytes of record, 4 to 8 of table and its share of a run of 8 to 20 bytes, and reads its memo words through the record: a record of 32 bytes with its key and its memo words is one cache line at best and two at worst (an \code{\verb~|int[]~} is not aligned to cache lines; by the arithmetic of its header every second record straddles two lines in three placements of four, which was not measured). Smaller records, or old records moved next to each other by what finds them together, are a change of the value layout (\code{\verb~|Layout~}, the ISA), not of the tables, and were not tried.}
\li{\strong{The bound is the machine's own session, not the worker's overlays.} The sweep raised the driver's cap with the machine's, so the term and level overlays of a worker grew too. With the driver's cap left at its 4 MiB and only the machine's raised to 2 MiB, cslib is 3.8 \% slower than at the default (3.4 \% with the driver's cap at 8 MiB) and Mathlib's process CPU is 2.8 \% higher (2.2 \%), in each of three rounds; with a machine cap of 4 MiB under the driver's 4 MiB, 5.1 \% and 3.7 \% (section 6.3).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. What was not measured}

\ul{
\li{Anything on x86-64, the arena's architecture: its second-level cache and its last-level cache have other sizes, and the edge that the cap sits on (section 2.1) moves with them.}
\li{A quiet host. Every window of this report was taken while other packages and this report's gates were computing on the other cores (section 1.3); the guard is three interleaved rounds per corpus and launcher and the CPU ratio of every round is given, but the absolute times are not those of the benchmark tables, and the JVM launcher's window was disturbed enough that its ratios of the passes are less certain than its CPU ratios.}
\li{The young and old tables with each part taken out: the base sizes alone were measured on the unchanged base (section 3.6), the young and old tables with the former base sizes on navier-stokes-euler only (the process CPU of a pass 0.980 and 0.970 of the base's in two windows of two and three rounds), the batches of the moves only as lines of two profiles, the size hint of the old tables on navier-stokes-euler at two caps (section 3.4).}
\li{Init with the young and old tables against Init with the sizes alone over more than three rounds (section 5.3): the one window that compared them shows longer passes at the same CPU, and a longer window was not obtained.}
\li{Why the base's debug mode \code{memocheck} declines one declaration of Init when the session cap is 16 MiB (section 4.4).}
\li{Other sizes of the young tables than 2^15 and 2^16 slots for the records with 2^12 and 2^13 pairs (2^17 slots with 2^14 pairs was measured on the base only: 1.7 \% of the CPU on navier-stokes-euler against 2.7 \% for the sizes chosen).}
\li{The instruction count of the arena (its metric): not taken. The change removes rehash loops and adds a second probe for keys made of old things; the counters say how often (section 2.4), no count was run.}
\li{The one-worker cost of a tick, and the unit level against gruszka: the largest proofs of navier-stokes-euler, where rep-P11 found the tick dearest, were not profiled alone with the change. reachability, whose two units are sessions of that kind, is the evidence here (15 \% of its pass).}
\li{A cap between 1 and 2 MiB, and a cap below 1 MiB with the new tables (rep-P11 had 256 KiB 1 to 3 \% faster on cslib and 5 \% slower on navier-stokes-euler on the old ones).}
\li{The optional Oracle GraalVM build (no Oracle GraalVM was used), and the Truffle tier.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. The patch, and what an integrator must know}

\ul{
\li{\strong{One patch} for the tables, against the main tree of 2026-11-25 (it applies without a failed hunk): \code{\verb~|Machine.java~} (the tables, the moves, the reset, the base sizes), \code{\verb~|Values.java~}, \code{\verb~|Runs.java~}, \code{\verb~|Pairs.java~} (the probes), \code{\verb~|Debug.java~} (the pair entry of \code{memocheck} and \code{shortcheck}; the audit), one line of \src{rlvm-machine/h10/force-inline.txt}, two paragraphs of \src{rlvm-machine/README.md}, one line of \src{rlvm-machine/OWNERS.tsv}, and the tests (six changed classes, one new). No other module is touched: not \code{rlvm-level}, not the loader, not the driver, not the build. \strong{There is no second patch}: the cap does not change (section 6).}
\li{\strong{A smaller alternative}, for an integrator who wants the gain of the small-unit corpora without the new tables: the base sizes alone, two changed lines of \code{\verb~|Machine.java~} (2^16 record slots and 2^13 pairs as the defaults of \code{\verb~|rlvm.machine.sizes~}). It was not built as an executable of its own: it is the base executable with that setting, whose counters are identical on Init and navier-stokes-euler and whose timings are sections 3.6 and 5.3. It gives the base's one table per kind a size that a session of 1 MiB does not outgrow, and does nothing for reachability, con-leche and cedar. The two patches change the same line; the main patch contains the alternative.}
\li{\strong{The inline list.} The entry \code{\verb~|rlvm.machine.Values.closureOverNew~} names a method that no longer exists; the patch replaces it by \code{\verb~|rlvm.machine.Values.closureNew~}, the method that took its place (called from one site, as its predecessor was). Nothing else in the list changes; the build reports 269 forced and 81 never-inlined methods as before. None of the new methods is named \code{\verb~|..Slow~}, so none enters the never-inline list by its name.}
\li{\strong{A new precondition of the probes}: a key is made of records and runs that exist in the session. The machine never gives a probe anything else; a test that calls \code{\verb~|Pairs~}, \code{\verb~|Values~} or \code{\verb~|Runs~} with invented record refs must make that many records first (two tests did, and were changed). The reference probes of the test tree search both tables and have no such precondition.}
\li{\strong{Who reads the tables directly} must know there are two: \code{\verb~|m.vtab~}, \code{\verb~|m.rtab~} and \code{\verb~|m.pair~} are the young tables only, and \code{\verb~|m.plog~} is the log of the old pair table only. The pairs of a session in the order they were made are \code{\verb~|m.pairWord(i, word)~} for \code{\verb~|i~} below \code{\verb~|m.pairUsed~}.}
\li{\strong{Settings.} \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.sizes~} now gives the sizes of the young tables (default \code{\verb~|14,16,16,14,13,10~}); \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.oldhint=off~} makes an old table start at the young table's size (the A/B of section 3.4); \code{\verb~|-Drlvm.machine.tableaudit=true~} runs the audit at every reset (for gates; a session of 20,000 records costs a map of that size).}
\li{\strong{After applying}: the machine's fast group (424 tests), the gate of the default configuration through the executable and through the jars, and one audit run of a large corpus through the JVM launcher. The journal's design notes on the session tables ([warm sessions and the session cap](design-warm-sessions), [the cost of one machine operation](prob-28KQ)) describe one table per kind and base sizes of 2^15 and 2^12; they were not edited by this package.}
\li{\strong{If another package of the wave gives the executable huge pages}: the old tables and the slabs of a large session are what they would help most; the sweep of section 6 is then worth repeating at 2 and 4 MiB on navier-stokes-euler, where the caps are already level.}
}
}
